<!-- ============ 8 · FAQ ============ -->
<section id="faq">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sh">
      <span class="k">Questions</span>
      <h2 data-reveal>Asked and answered.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="faq">
      <details data-reveal><summary>Are the duas authentic?</summary><p class="fa-p">Yes. Qur'anic text is transcribed from the Uthmani mushaf and verified against Tanzil, never typed by hand and never generated. Every card names its exact source, and if a dua cannot carry a checkable source it does not enter the deck. Scholar review is the last gate before public release: every card records whether it has been reviewed and by whom, and today that field reads pending.</p></details>
      <details data-reveal><summary>Is Baik really free?</summary><p class="fa-p">Every dua, its Arabic, transliteration, meaning and source, is free forever. Premium only unlocks tools around the duas: audio, unlimited saves, guided journeys, offline packs. The religious content itself is never behind a price.</p></details>
      <details data-reveal><summary>Do I need to download the app?</summary><p class="fa-p">No. The web version works in any browser today, free. The mobile app is on its way; it adds offline access, notifications and audio downloads. Use the web now, take the app when it lands.</p></details>
      <details data-reveal><summary>When does the app launch?</summary><p class="fa-p">It is in development. The first 1,000 users get the founding member lifetime price of RM129.90. Open the web app and you will see the plan and progress inside.</p></details>
      <details data-reveal><summary>What about my privacy?</summary><p class="fa-p">No advertisements, no selling of personal data. Your notes, saves and reading habits stay yours. Location is only ever requested for Qibla and prayer times, and only when you ask for them.</p></details>
      <details data-reveal><summary>Bahasa Melayu?</summary><p class="fa-p">Ya, insya-Allah. The deck was born bilingual; the BM layer ships with the language toggle. English is first while the Malay translations go through review.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

